# eRPC multi-provider proxy for Optimism mainnet (chain 10).
#
# **Current shape: 2-of-3 consensus across publicnode + zan(KEYED) + alchemy(KEYED).**
#
# Iteration history:
#  - #130 deploy: tried publicnode + official-op + ankr-op. official-op
#    (mainnet.optimism.io) → HTTP 429 from Mac mini IP. ankr-op needs
#    paid API key. Dropped both, swapped in tenderly + 1rpc.
#  - #138 deploy: publicnode + tenderly + 1rpc (2-of-3, threshold=2).
#  - #144 deploy: bumped to 3-of-3 after Codex retro-audit caught the
#    CF-collusion poisoning attack (2 CF-fronted upstreams returning
#    the same forged view satisfy quorum 2-of-3 → poison authoritative).
#  - **2026-05-20 (this PR)**: dropped 1rpc-op entirely. eRPC's URL
#    parser strips the `/op` path segment from `https://1rpc.io/op`,
#    sending requests to 1rpc's landing page instead of the OP RPC.
#    Confirmed via the parsed-config dump:
#      "endpoint":"https://1rpc.io#redacted=..." (no path)
#    1rpc consistently failed → `n/a = ErrUpstreamsExhausted` on every
#    consensus call → 3-of-3 was unsatisfiable → driver couldn't boot.
#
# **Final config: 3 upstreams, agreementThreshold=2 (2-of-3 quorum).**
# 2026-07-23 keyed migration: replaced the free publicnode(Cloudflare) + dead
# self-host(100.77.53.81) with two KEYED providers after a free-tier-429 outage.
# 2026-07-30 keyed REBUILD (this change): both prior keyed lanes died the same day
# (validationcloud 401 "api client is disabled", blockdaemon 429) and every other
# keyed provider we held was monthly-quota-exhausted (alchemy, drpc) — a billing
# failure, not a config one. The quorum was rebuilt around a NEW keyed lane (zan,
# registered account) plus two free lanes. Full per-upstream rationale and the
# measured per-method coverage live in the `upstreams:` block below.
#
#   publicnode-op       optimism-rpc.publicnode.com   Allnodes
#                                                      CDN: Cloudflare (the ONE permitted)
#                                                      auth: NONE (free public tier)
#                                                      gap: no eth_getTransactionReceipt
#                                                           (archive-gated)
#   zan-op              api.zan.top                   ZAN (Ant Digital Technologies)
#                                                      CDN: none detected (NON-Cloudflare)
#                                                      auth: KEYED (${ZAN_API_KEY}, URL path)
#                                                      serves ALL protected methods
#   alchemy-op          opt-mainnet.g.alchemy.com     Alchemy
#                                                      CDN: istio-envoy, no CF headers
#                                                           (NON-Cloudflare; measured 2026-08-09)
#                                                      auth: KEYED (${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}, URL path)
#                                                      serves ALL protected methods
#
# **Threat model — 2-of-3 over 3 distinct failure domains, with the hard
# invariant that AT MOST ONE upstream is Cloudflare-fronted (now publicnode):**
#
#  - Cloudflare compromise → only publicnode is hijacked. zan (non-CF) and
#    alchemy (istio-envoy/GCP) return the real answer → dispute → returnError →
#    driver does NOT settle against forged state.
#  - ZAN OR Alchemy compromise → the other two form quorum on the real
#    answer → safe. NO 2-upstream subset shares one CDN/DNS, so a single-provider
#    compromise can never satisfy quorum alone. (This property survived the
#    2026-07-30 keyed withdrawal AND the 2026-08-09 tenderly retirement: it is
#    why alchemy was chosen over blockdaemon, which measured Cloudflare-fronted
#    on 2026-08-09 — cf-ray present — and would have made TWO CF lanes.)
#  - ⚠️ THE PRECONDITION for the line above: that claim holds ONLY where all three
#    lanes can actually ANSWER the method. It is not automatic — it is a property
#    to re-check whenever a lane changes. Review finding 2026-07-30 (P1): while zan
#    ran on its unregistered tier it hard-rejected eth_call / eth_getLogs /
#    eth_estimateGas, so only two lanes responded. Two responders still SATISFY
#    agreementThreshold:2, so a hostile lane produced a DISPUTE rather than
#    low-participants — and disputeBehavior:preferBlockHeadLeader then selects
#    whichever upstream advertises the freshest head, which the attacker controls.
#    Its forged value would win with nothing agreeing with it. Keying the zan lane
#    restored three capable lanes for EVERY protected method, which is the real fix
#    (an interim fail-closed "Block T" rule was removed once it landed).
#    RULE OF THUMB when editing this file: for each protected method, count the
#    lanes that can actually serve it. If the count equals agreementThreshold, the
#    single-compromise protection is GONE for that method — either add a capable
#    lane or force that method to disputeBehavior:returnError.
#    Still true today for eth_getTransactionReceipt (publicnode is archive-gated,
#    so only zan + alchemy serve it) — which is why the receipt rule below is
#    already disputeBehavior:returnError, i.e. fail-closed rather than tie-broken.
#  - Two independent failure domains compromised at once: nation-state class,
#    out of scope.
#  - All three returning the same answer IS the canonical chain state
#    (independent operators agreeing on block data is the correct case).
#
# Availability: 2 of 3 must both respond AND agree; a single-upstream blip is
# tolerated (the other two form quorum). Fail-closed below quorum:
# lowParticipantsBehavior:returnError → driver pauses rather than settling on
# unverified state. Trade-off accepted: "rather not settle than settle against
# forged state." Standard posture for any DEX moving real money.
#
# Rationale (replace-not-expand): N=3 keeps the 2-of-3 invariant defensible;
# expanding N>3 under eRPC's random sampling (maxParticipants:3) would silently
# weaken the model to "attacker needs ≥2 of N". TWO upstreams are KEYED (zan +
# alchemy) since 2026-08-09 — the "second keyed lane" hardening item left open by
# the 2026-07-30 rebuild is now closed. The keyed pair can satisfy 2-of-3 by
# itself, so the free lane (publicnode) flaking at the tip no longer collapses
# the quorum. The common cause across the 07-23, 07-30 and 08-09 outages
# (each with a DIFFERENT lane topology — see this file's history sections) was
# that quorum depended on rate-capped free lanes answering under load; a load
# spike dropped participants below threshold → lowParticipants:returnError
# (driver pauses; safe but unavailable). A keyed pair removes free-lane
# availability from the quorum-critical path. Per-block consensus equality is
# sound on OP: post-Bedrock block contents are deterministic per L2 block
# number; only tip drift on "latest" varies, handled by `enforceHighestBlock`.
#
# Endpoint exposed: http://rpc-proxy:4000/main/evm/10

logLevel: warn  # was info; info logs 1 line/RPC req+resp -> filled the docker-VM disk (2026-06-15 incident). warn still surfaces consensus disputes / upstream-exhausted.

projects:
  - id: main

    networks:
      - architecture: evm
        evm:
          chainId: 10
          integrity:
            enforceHighestBlock: true
            enforceNonNullTaggedBlocks: true
        # Consensus failsafe: defeats fork-view-poisoning on state-reads.
        # Each free provider is unauthenticated HTTPS — a hostile provider
        # (or MITM via CA compromise / DNS hijack) could otherwise serve a
        # self-consistent fork that the autopilot signs settlements against.
        # 2-of-3 agreement on the methods that drive solver behavior closes
        # that hole.
        failsafe:
          # Critical reads: state-dependent calls that gate solver behavior
          # and settlement validity. eth_getTransactionByHash is included
          # because the response carries forkable fields (blockHash,
          # blockNumber); a hostile upstream on the non-consensus "*" path
          # could otherwise mislead the autopilot's settlement-watcher.
          # Network-level timeout 12s gives 2s headroom over the slowest
          # upstream-level timeout (~10s) — sharp-edges follow-up #6.
          #
          # Method list (Codex retro-audit, 2026-05-20):
          # - `eth_estimateGas`: a forged-low estimate yields txs that
          #   revert (gas-only loss bounded by the 5 gwei cap from #131,
          #   but still budget-wasteful).
          # - `eth_feeHistory`: feeds into EIP-1559 fee predictions;
          #   manipulation lets a hostile upstream steer submission gas.
          #
          # 2026-05-20 evening — DROPPED `eth_getBlockByNumber` and
          # `eth_getBlockByHash` from strict consensus. They were the
          # dominant source of `ErrConsensusDispute` (95+ disputes in
          # 2-minute windows post-deploy) because the two upstreams hold
          # slightly different views of `"latest"` at any wall-clock
          # microsecond — pure tip-drift, no security signal. Block reads
          # by NUMBER (not tag) would be deterministic but the driver
          # mostly queries `"latest"`. Removing these methods from
          # consensus drops to the `*` catch-all rule below (retry, no
          # consensus). Per-block-content integrity is still enforced
          # via `enforceHighestBlock: true` + `enforceNonNullTaggedBlocks:
          # true` at the network level above. Settlement-integrity
          # methods (`eth_call`, `eth_getLogs`, `eth_getTransactionReceipt`,
          # `eth_getTransactionByHash`) keep 2-of-3 consensus.
          #
          # 2026-05-20 evening (2) — DROPPED `eth_getBalance` from strict
          # consensus too. Tenderly's free public Gateway hit per-IP
          # rate-limit (HTTP 429 ErrEndpointCapacityExceeded) under
          # sustained polling from driver+orderbook+autopilot. (Residual
          # since closed in 2026-05-20 evening (3) below.)
          #
          # 2026-05-20 evening (3) — ADDED self-hosted ophis-self-op
          # as the 3rd upstream. A self-hosted full node reached via
          # Tailscale, fully synced, controlled by us. This removes the
          # Tenderly rate-limit bottleneck (we poll our own node freely)
          # AND adds a 3rd fully-independent failure domain (separate
          # network operator — not CF, not Google).
          #
          # Restored: `eth_getBalance` back to strict consensus. The
          # rate-limit reason for demotion is no longer a constraint.
          #
          # Bumped: agreementThreshold 2 → 3 (every upstream must agree).
          # Threat model now (3 distinct DNS/operator/network):
          #   - CF DNS compromise: hijacks only publicnode → tenderly +
          #     self return truth → dispute → returnError → safe
          #   - Google DNS compromise: hijacks only tenderly → publicnode
          #     + self → safe
          #   - Aleph compromise (someone gets into our VM): hijacks only
          #     self → publicnode + tenderly → safe
          #   - All 3 simultaneously: requires owning CF + Google + Aleph
          #     infrastructures → nation-state++ class, out of scope.
          #
          # This is the strongest posture achievable today. The CF-DNS
          # concentration residual (documented through #144→#148 iterations)
          # is now fully closed.
          # 2026-05-20 — root-cause REVISED during task #112 investigation.
          # The Phase 3.1 swap failure was NOT a container→Tailscale
          # reachability issue (a one-shot alpine container on the same
          # docker network successfully called the self-hosted endpoint). The
          # actual cause: eRPC's punishMisbehavior policy was kicking the
          # self-hosted upstream into a 5-min sit-out every time it had
          # minor tip-drift (5 disputes in 10min → punishment). With OP's
          # 2s block time, tip-drift between independently-operated
          # indexers is the natural state — disputeThreshold:5 punishes
          # normal operation.
          #
          # ============================================================
          # 2026-05-20 (task #119) — SPLIT disputeThreshold BY METHOD.
          # ============================================================
          #
          # Why the single-bucket threshold:100 was unsafe to keep:
          # a single global threshold of 100 means a hostile upstream
          # can return up to 99 forged answers per 10-min window on the
          # most security-critical methods (eth_call, eth_getBalance)
          # before catching a 5-min sit-out — sustained ~95% DoS duty
          # cycle on the path that gates settlement. Phase 4 audit N1
          # (Codex + sharp-edges, 2026-05-20) flagged this as
          # defense-in-depth-required pre-meaningful-TVL.
          #
          # Split into two consensus blocks by threat surface:
          #
          # **Block A — deterministic-state methods (LOW threshold)**:
          # eth_call, eth_getBalance, eth_getCode, eth_getStorageAt.
          # These are pure state lookups at a specified block tag.
          #
          # `enforceHighestBlock: true` (set at the network level above)
          # gates routing to only-fully-caught-up upstreams — it does
          # NOT coordinate the participants to query a specific block
          # number. Two upstreams 1 block apart on "latest" will still
          # return two state snapshots; only the behind-tip one gets
          # routed-around. Drift IS real on this bucket.
          # `eth_getTransactionCount` is deliberately NOT in Block A:
          # the `pending` block tag is mempool-dependent, and the
          # driver's submission mempool is pinned to publicnode-op
          # alone — the self-hosted op-node will not see pending txs
          # before the sequencer gossips them back, so EIP-7702
          # delegation-setup ceremonies (90s of pending-tag polling)
          # would cordon self-hosted within seconds of every boot.
          # Moved to Block B where threshold:100 absorbs that burst.
          #
          # threshold:60 catches hostile sustained divergence ~1.7× faster
          # than the previous threshold:100 while preserving 3× headroom
          # over the live ~20 disputes/10min baseline (driven primarily
          # by ophis-self-op tip-drift on "latest" tags). Sharp-
          # edges finding 1 (2026-05-20): the 1.5× headroom of threshold:30
          # was insufficient for bootstrap-burst + OP-sequencer-hiccup
          # scenarios where all 3 upstreams snap back to consensus at
          # different microseconds. Threshold:60 is the floor; raise to
          # 100 (and accept the DoS-window cost) only if false-ejection
          # alerts fire repeatedly on a healthy upstream over a 24h
          # window.
          #
          # **Block B — tip-lag-tolerant observation methods (HIGH
          # threshold)**: eth_getLogs, eth_getTransactionReceipt,
          # eth_getTransactionByHash, eth_estimateGas, eth_feeHistory,
          # eth_getTransactionCount.
          # These have natural disagreement floors driven by indexer
          # propagation lag near the chain tip (e.g. just-landed
          # Settlement txs may not be indexed by all upstreams
          # simultaneously; estimateGas has provider-implementation
          # variance; feeHistory percentiles differ slightly between
          # providers; getTransactionCount with `pending` tag is
          # mempool-dependent and only the publicnode-op mempool is
          # authoritative for our submission path). threshold:100
          # absorbs this baseline drift while still catching sustained
          # malicious answers — each forged dispute still triggers
          # consensus failure → returnError → driver fails closed at
          # request time. The threshold only controls whether we keep
          # retrying that upstream over time.
          #
          # **Block C** is the existing catch-all retry (no consensus).
          #
          # Empirical baseline (live OP mainnet eRPC, ~1h window
          # 2026-05-20 evening):
          #   eth_call:                      2.09% dispute rate
          #   eth_feeHistory:                1.64% dispute rate
          #   eth_getTransactionReceipt:     0.25% dispute rate
          #   eth_getLogs:                   0.12% dispute rate
          # The Block A threshold of 60 leaves 3× headroom for eth_call
          # (the most-disputed method in that bucket). Operational reality
          # test: redeploy → monitor erpc_upstream_cordoned for false
          # ejections in the first hour. If false-ejection fires more
          # than once per 30 minutes on a healthy upstream, raise to 100
          # (matching Block B) and accept the DoS-window cost.
          #
          # **Pairs with new Prometheus alerts** (observability/alerts.yml):
          # - OphisOpErpcUpstreamCordoned (per-upstream sat-out detection)
          # - OphisOpErpcUpstreamBlockHeadLagging (chronic-lag root cause)
          # - OphisOpErpcCordonMetricAbsent (silent-monitoring-gap guard)

          # === Block A: Deterministic-state methods (LOW threshold) ===
          - matchMethod: "eth_call|eth_getBalance|eth_getCode|eth_getStorageAt"
            timeout:
              duration: 12s
            consensus:
              maxParticipants: 3
              # 2-of-3 across fully-independent failure domains:
              # - publicnode-op (Allnodes; CF DNS)
              # - zan-op (Ant Digital Technologies; non-CF)
              # - alchemy-op (Alchemy; istio-envoy/GCP, non-CF)
              agreementThreshold: 2
              # Tip-drift fix (#476): on OP's ~2s blocks publicnode + self
              # frequently sit 1 block apart on `latest`-tagged reads — a
              # DISPUTE (both upstreams UP, values disagree) that the old
              # returnError failed ~30-50% of the time. preferBlockHeadLeader
              # breaks the tie by freshest block; it still requires the
              # participants to have responded, so it is not a 1-of-N bypass.
              disputeBehavior: preferBlockHeadLeader
              # lowParticipants stays FAIL-CLOSED (Codex #476 P1): when fewer
              # than agreementThreshold upstreams return a valid response (an
              # outage, not a disagreement), refuse rather than serve the
              # lone freshest 1-of-3 — a single hijacked/stale fresh upstream
              # must never supply settlement/quote state unchecked.
              lowParticipantsBehavior: returnError
              # No `ignoreFields` here — see HL eRPC Codex Cyber finding #1.
              # Allowing field-level skip lets a hostile upstream forge the
              # skipped field while matching every other field; consensus
              # passes spuriously.
              punishMisbehavior:
                # TIGHT threshold for security-critical state lookups.
                # 3× headroom over the empirical 20 disputes/10min
                # baseline on eth_call. A hostile upstream catches a 5-min
                # sit-out after 60 forged answers (vs 100 in the old
                # single-bucket config) — 1.7× DoS-window reduction on
                # the settlement-gating path. Sharp-edges (2026-05-20)
                # bumped this from 30 → 60 to absorb bootstrap-burst
                # scenarios where 3 upstreams snap back to consensus at
                # different microseconds during driver redeploy.
                disputeThreshold: 60
                disputeWindow: 10m
                sitOutPenalty: 5m

          # === Block B: Tip-lag-sensitive observation methods (HIGH threshold) ===
          #
          # 2026-05-21 follow-up: REMOVED eth_getTransactionReceipt from
          # the Block B consensus regex. The 200-threshold bump on 2026-05-20
          # was not enough — `OphisOpErpcUpstreamCordoned` fired 3 more
          # times within hours of the deploy, each time accumulating
          # ~12,000 `response_type="empty"` misbehavior events on
          # ophis-self-op before the eviction. Root cause is
          # structural: op-reth's receipt indexing is materially slower
          # than publicnode + tenderly's hosted indexers (likely they
          # serve from a denormalized receipt cache rather than
          # re-traversing the trie). During Settlement-broadcast bursts
          # the driver polls fresh receipts every block tick; self-hosted
          # returns empty for a few seconds longer than the others, ends
          # up the lone dissenter on every poll, and the dispute counter
          # accumulates faster than ANY threshold we could tune around
          # without effectively disabling the policy.
          #
          # Trade-off accepted: eth_getTransactionReceipt now falls
          # through to Block C (catch-all retry, no consensus). The
          # protection we lose is "a hostile upstream forging
          # receipt-exists/receipt-missing to mislead the autopilot
          # settlement-watcher". The watcher cross-checks via eth_call
          # against the on-chain Settlement contract (which IS still
          # under Block A consensus at threshold:60), so a forged
          # receipt alone cannot cause settlement-state divergence —
          # only the eth_call against Settlement's `filledAmount` is
          # authoritative. Receipt confirmation is best-effort UX, not
          # a security primitive.
          #
          # If a future version of op-reth speeds up receipt indexing
          # (or we switch to op-erigon / op-geth on the VM), re-add
          # eth_getTransactionReceipt to this regex and bump back.
          - matchMethod: "eth_getLogs|eth_getTransactionByHash|eth_estimateGas|eth_feeHistory|eth_getTransactionCount"
            timeout:
              duration: 12s
            consensus:
              maxParticipants: 3
              agreementThreshold: 2
              # Same tip-drift posture as Block A: dispute breaks ties by
              # freshest block, but lowParticipants stays fail-closed so an
              # outage never downgrades to 1-of-3 (Codex #476 P1).
              disputeBehavior: preferBlockHeadLeader
              lowParticipantsBehavior: returnError
              punishMisbehavior:
                # 200 was sized to absorb the receipt burst — without
                # that method here, the remaining members have much
                # lower dispute floors, but we keep 100 (the original
                # value) as a defensive cap. Drift between publicnode +
                # tenderly + self on `eth_getLogs` near tip is ~10
                # disputes / 10min historically; threshold:100 gives 10×
                # headroom.
                disputeThreshold: 100
                disputeWindow: 10m
                sitOutPenalty: 5m

          # === eth_getTransactionReceipt: consensus WITHOUT punishMisbehavior ===
          # Settlement-authoritative — the driver derives Executed/Reverted from
          # this receipt (driver/domain/mempools.rs), so a single forged/hidden
          # receipt could poison the settle() outcome. It needs 2-of-3 agreement.
          # BUT it gets its OWN rule with NO punishMisbehavior: the self-hosted
          # op-reth returns empty receipts a few seconds longer than the hosted
          # indexers during settlement broadcast, so under the punished Block A/B
          # rules it disputed -> cordoned ophis-self-op (Codex #465/#466, ~12k
          # empty-misbehavior events). With no punishment the lag merely yields a
          # transient returnError, which the driver treats as Pending and keeps
          # polling (fail-safe: it can delay confirmation but never accept a
          # forged receipt or evict the node). Once the node's receipt lag is
          # fixed (op-erigon/op-geth), fold this into a punished block.
          - matchMethod: "eth_getTransactionReceipt"
            timeout:
              duration: 12s
            consensus:
              maxParticipants: 3
              agreementThreshold: 2
              disputeBehavior: returnError
              lowParticipantsBehavior: returnError

          # === Block C: Catch-all retry (no consensus) ===
          # Block-stream polling, balance reads on non-consensus paths.
          # Tx submission routes via driver's pinned [[submission.mempool]]
          # block (single endpoint, nonce-coherent).
          - matchMethod: "*"
            timeout:
              duration: 12s
            retry:
              maxAttempts: 3
              delay: 150ms
              backoffMaxDelay: 1s
              backoffFactor: 1.5
              jitter: 50ms

    # OP block time is ~2s post-Bedrock; align tip-block tracking.
    upstreamDefaults:
      evm:
        # 2026-07-31: 5s -> 10s. eRPC's own block-head poller accounted for
        # ~0.5 polls/s per upstream (about 21% of all traffic to the keyed lane)
        # and is pure infrastructure — unlike the autopilot's poll-interval it
        # has no product-latency cost, so it is the cheapest saving available.
        #
        # Deliberately conservative rather than 15s+: `enforceHighestBlock: true`
        # relies on the poller's view of the tip, and a staler view means more
        # 1-block tip drift between upstreams, which is what disputeBehavior:
        # preferBlockHeadLeader has to resolve. Doubling is a measurable step;
        # check erpc_consensus_errors_total{error="dispute"} before going further.
        #
        # NOTE: measured polling ran at ~2s despite the 5s setting, so this poller
        # is substantially request-driven and the interval is an upper bound, not
        # a guarantee. Treat any saving here as a bonus and verify it empirically.
        statePollerInterval: 10s
        statePollerDebounce: 1s

    upstreams:
      # ─── Keyed-pair consensus set (2026-08-09 incident) ───
      # publicnode(free, CF) + zan(KEYED) + alchemy(KEYED). This executes the
      # RESTORE PATH left open by the 2026-07-30 stopgap ("put ≥2 keyed lanes
      # back"): alchemy's free quota reset 2026-08-01 as predicted and measured
      # clean on 2026-08-09.
      #
      # WHY 2026-08-09 BROKE (tenderly retired): a user swap on OP sat unsolved
      # >30min and expired. Autopilot logged NoSolutions from all 12 solvers on
      # every auction; rpc-proxy logged ErrConsensusLowParticipants on eth_call:
      #   zan-op          OK (lone valid participant)
      #   tenderly-op     -32005 rate limit (x-tdly-limit: 20 — its DOCUMENTED cap;
      #                   named cause of the 07-23 and 07-30 incidents too)
      #   publicnode-op   ErrUpstreamBlockUnavailable (tip-pinned eth_call, lags
      #                   ~1 block on 2s blocktime)
      # 1 valid < agreementThreshold:2 → returnError → solvers get no state →
      # NoSolutions → orders expire unsettled. The stack "looked healthy" for 9
      # idle days because tenderly's cap only bites under real auction load.
      # Last settled block before the fix: 154955332 (~2026-07-31).
      #
      # Candidates measured 2026-08-09 (all 10 protected methods + 12-shot burst):
      #   alchemy         ALL OK, 12/12 burst, server: istio-envoy (NON-CF)  ← chosen
      #   validationcloud null responses, 0/12 — client still disabled
      #   blockdaemon     10/12 burst BUT now Cloudflare-fronted (cf-ray) →
      #                   would pair a 2nd CF lane with publicnode → disqualified
      #
      # CDN-DIVERSITY INVARIANT — PRESERVED. "≤1 Cloudflare-fronted upstream per
      # quorum" still holds: publicnode is the ONE CF lane; zan (no CF headers)
      # and alchemy (istio-envoy/GCP) are two non-CF failure domains, so a single
      # CDN compromise still cannot forge a 2-of-3 quorum.
      #
      # Hosts are LITERAL so the CI guard's urlsplit can pin the failure domain.
      # TWO ${...KEY} envsubst secrets (ZAN_API_KEY, ALCHEMY_API_KEY) — erpc.yaml
      # is in PK_BEARING_NAMES so the rendered file lands on the RAM-disk.
      - id: publicnode-op
        # Cloudflare-fronted (Allnodes) — the ONE permitted CF lane here.
        # Fastest of the three (median 54ms) and the most reliable for the pricing
        # path. Measured 2026-07-30: 12/12 burst, eth_call / eth_getLogs /
        # eth_estimateGas / eth_feeHistory all OK.
        # ⚠️ CANNOT serve eth_getTransactionReceipt: returns -32602 "Archive requests
        # require a personal token". That is why BOTH lanes below are keyed and
        # receipt-capable — the strict receipt rule needs two (zan + alchemy).
        endpoint: https://optimism-rpc.publicnode.com
        failsafe:
          - matchMethod: "*"
            timeout:
              duration: 8s
            retry:
              maxAttempts: 3
              delay: 200ms
              backoffMaxDelay: 1s
              backoffFactor: 1.5
              jitter: 50ms

      - id: zan-op
        # KEYED (registered account, 2026-07-30) and NON-Cloudflare (no cf-ray, no CF
        # server header) — together with alchemy this is what keeps the ≤1-CF
        # invariant intact.
        #
        # Measured on the KEYED endpoint 2026-07-30: ALL TEN guard-protected methods
        # OK (eth_call, eth_getLogs, eth_estimateGas, eth_getBalance, eth_getCode,
        # eth_getStorageAt, eth_feeHistory, eth_getTransactionCount,
        # eth_getTransactionByHash, eth_getTransactionReceipt) and 30/30 on an
        # unpaced eth_call burst. chainId verified 0xa.
        #
        # This SUPERSEDES the unregistered tier, which hard-blocked eth_call /
        # eth_getLogs / eth_estimateGas (-32012, "not available for unregistered
        # accounts"). That gap left those three methods with only two capable lanes,
        # which was a real vulnerability: two responders still satisfy
        # agreementThreshold:2, so a hostile lane produced a DISPUTE rather than
        # low-participants, and Blocks A/B's disputeBehavior:preferBlockHeadLeader
        # would then select whichever upstream advertised the freshest head — an
        # attacker-controlled value with nothing agreeing with it. Keying this lane
        # restores three capable lanes for every protected method, so an honest
        # majority can always outvote a single hostile provider, and the interim
        # "Block T" fail-closed rule was removed with it.
        #
        # Host is LITERAL so the CI guard's urlsplit can pin the failure domain; only
        # ${ZAN_API_KEY} resolves from .env via render-configs.sh envsubst — NEVER
        # committed. erpc.yaml is in PK_BEARING_NAMES, so the rendered file carrying
        # this key lands on the RAM-disk, not the SSD.
        #
        # WHY NOT 1rpc: it reaches the RPC fine now (the 2026-05-20 path-stripping
        # bug noted in this file's header is fixed) BUT its free tier dies under the
        # stack's per-block state-poller load — eRPC logged ErrEndpointBillingIssue
        # "You've reached the usage limit for your current plan" and receipts
        # measured 0/12 THROUGH THE PROXY. WHY NOT drpc: keyless tier returns "You
        # reached Public endpoint rate limit" (code 15) on eth_call and receipts
        # under load, which failed the receipt rule outright (ErrConsensusDispute).
        #
        # WHY NOT nodies (#898), which this change supersedes: nodies is a fine
        # endpoint in isolation — measured 2026-07-30 at op-pokt.nodies.app it was
        # 12/12 on a burst and served eth_call, eth_feeHistory AND receipts. But it
        # is Cloudflare-fronted, so pairing it with publicnode would put TWO CF lanes
        # in the quorum and break the ≤1-CF invariant; and while it was live in the
        # 07-29..07-30 config, eRPC logged it as ErrEndpointCapacityExceeded, "Rate
        # limit exceeded on Nodies public endpoints", under the per-block state
        # poller.
        # FAIRNESS CAVEAT: during that window its two co-lanes were official-op (hard
        # 429) and rate-capped tenderly, so nodies was absorbing the whole stack's
        # load plus retry amplification — harsher than it would face in a healthy
        # trio. It is therefore a REASONABLE CANDIDATE to revisit (it would give
        # receipts a 3rd lane), but it would have to displace publicnode to keep the
        # CF invariant, and publicnode is the lane with sustained clean-load evidence.
        # Swapping a proven-live lane for an unproven one mid-incident was not worth
        # the risk; revisit deliberately, and verify receipts THROUGH THE PROXY.
        endpoint: https://api.zan.top/node/v1/opt/mainnet/${ZAN_API_KEY}
        failsafe:
          - matchMethod: "*"
            timeout:
              duration: 8s
            retry:
              maxAttempts: 3
              delay: 200ms
              backoffMaxDelay: 1s
              backoffFactor: 1.5
              jitter: 50ms

      - id: alchemy-op
        # KEYED (free tier, monthly quota resets on the 1st) and NON-Cloudflare
        # (server: istio-envoy, no cf-ray) — the second authenticated lane,
        # replacing tenderly-op on 2026-08-09 after tenderly's hard x-tdly-limit:20
        # cap broke the quorum under auction load for the third time (07-23,
        # 07-30, 08-09). Measured on the KEYED endpoint 2026-08-09: ALL TEN
        # guard-protected methods OK (including eth_getTransactionReceipt, which
        # publicnode can never serve) and 12/12 on an unpaced eth_call burst.
        # chainId verified 0xa.
        #
        # ⚠️ QUOTA COUPLING: ${ALCHEMY_API_KEY} is the ACCOUNT-WIDE alchemy key
        # (keychain: alchemy-api-key) — the same account whose L1 lane feeds the
        # rebate-indexer's mainnet polling. It monthly-quota-exhausted in July
        # (429 "Monthly capacity limit exceeded") precisely because multiple
        # consumers shared it. OphisOpErpcUpstreamCredentialFailing (alerts.yml,
        # PR #1016) is the tripwire for a repeat. ⚠️ IF IT FIRES, REPLACE THE
        # LANE IMMEDIATELY — the degraded state is NOT fail-closed for Block
        # A/B: zan + publicnode still make agreementThreshold:2, so disputes
        # tie-break via preferBlockHeadLeader across only TWO capable lanes —
        # the exact single-compromise hole documented in this file's 2026-07-30
        # P1 (a hostile lane's freshest-head answer wins with nothing agreeing).
        # Only receipts fail closed (zan alone < 2). Swap in a vetted keyed
        # lane rather than loosening the policy.
        endpoint: https://opt-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}
        failsafe:
          - matchMethod: "*"
            timeout:
              duration: 8s
            retry:
              maxAttempts: 3
              delay: 200ms
              backoffMaxDelay: 1s
              backoffFactor: 1.5
              jitter: 50ms

      # ─── Consensus set (2026-08-09): publicnode + zan + alchemy ───
      # 2-of-3, agreementThreshold=2, maxParticipants=3 — UNCHANGED. This incident
      # swapped only the tenderly endpoint for a keyed alchemy lane; no consensus
      # parameter was touched and every rule remains fail-closed. Exactly one
      # CF-fronted upstream (publicnode) → a single-CDN compromise still can't
      # poison a full quorum.
      #
      # Per-method quorum margin, re-measured 2026-08-09 on the KEYED alchemy lane:
      #   eth_call / eth_getLogs / eth_estimateGas ..................... 3 of 3 lanes
      #   eth_getCode / eth_getStorageAt / eth_getBalance / txCount .... 3 of 3 lanes
      #   eth_feeHistory / eth_getTransactionByHash .................... 3 of 3 lanes
      #   eth_getTransactionReceipt ................... 2 of 3 (publicnode archive-gated)
      #
      # RE-MEASURE THIS TABLE whenever a lane's credentials or tier change (the
      # 2026-07-30 review caught a stale table understating zan's coverage — a
      # wrong count here invites exactly the 2-lane preferBlockHeadLeader hole
      # this file's safety argument exists to prevent).
      #
      # eth_getTransactionReceipt is the only remaining thin method, and it is the
      # reason its rule is disputeBehavior:returnError rather than
      # preferBlockHeadLeader — with two responders a leader tie-break is
      # attacker-selectable, so it must fail closed instead. The RECEIPT rule needs
      # BOTH zan and alchemy to answer and agree; if either is rate-limited,
      # lowParticipantsBehavior:returnError makes it fail CLOSED (safe — the driver
      # refuses to conclude Executed/Reverted rather than guessing) while
      # settlement indexing stalls until it recovers.
      #
      # AVAILABILITY PROPERTY OF THE KEYED PAIR (why this set fixes 08-09): the
      # two keyed lanes alone satisfy agreementThreshold:2 for every protected
      # method, so publicnode tip-lag or free-tier throttling degrades nothing.
      # Under the single-keyed 07-30 set, quorum required at least one of two
      # rate-capped free lanes to answer — which is what failed under auction load
      # three times. Verify receipts THROUGH THE PROXY, not just /quote: with
      # agreementThreshold:2 a dead 3rd lane hides behind the other two satisfying
      # quorum (/quote stayed 10/10 green while receipts were 0/12 on 07-30).
      #
      # #841 self-host (46.255.204.207, op-reth) is now OPTIONAL, not load-bearing. If
      # re-added, keep the ≤1-CF-per-quorum invariant and either swap a lane (stay at 3)
      # or bump to 2-of-4 — the latter ALSO needs assert-erpc-failclosed.py's
      # EXPECTED_UPSTREAMS + EXPECTED_UPSTREAM_HOSTS updated. A self-hosted OP node
      # remains the structural fix for free-tier quota exposure overall.
      # tenderly is NOT a fallback quorum member: in a fixed 3-lane set any
      # swapped-in lane is load-bearing by construction, and tenderly's hard
      # x-tdly-limit:20 cap is the named cause of three outages (07-23, 07-30,
      # 08-09). Keep it only as a keyless DIAGNOSTIC probe endpoint. If a keyed
      # lane dies mid-cycle, re-vet a replacement against the full bar:
      # non-Cloudflare, receipt-capable, all 10 protected methods, sustained
      # burst through the proxy — not just a low-rate hand probe.
      # drpc / 1rpc / meowrpc / omnia / blockdaemon(2026-08-09) are disqualified as
      # ADDITIONAL lanes: all are Cloudflare-fronted, so pairing any of them with
      # publicnode would create the 2-CF quorum that defeats the fork-poisoning
      # threat model. validationcloud: client still disabled (null responses,
      # 0/12) as of 2026-08-09.

